WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.538
James: This is a video of me, falling in love.

00:00:16.538 --> 00:00:21.411
It’s only 9 seconds long, which is why we’re
having to loop it again and again.

00:00:21.411 --> 00:00:25.529
I didn’t know that much about it compared
to everything I know now.

00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:29.210
I barely even knew how to look at it properly.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:33.424
But I knew that it was beautiful, and that
I wanted to see more.

00:00:36.582 --> 00:00:41.540
Hank: That is James, who many of you know
as our master of microscopes.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:42.930
He is the scientist—

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.469
and I would also say the artist—

00:00:45.469 --> 00:00:51.228
behind just about everything we are able to
see in our collective journey through the microcosmos.

00:00:51.228 --> 00:00:54.355
Now some of you may know the story 
of how that journey got started.

00:00:54.355 --> 00:00:59.829
How I, Hank Green, saw James’ work online
and then reached out to him to see if he would

00:00:59.829 --> 00:01:02.357
want to start this channel together.

00:01:02.357 --> 00:01:07.747
When I saw the videos he posted, the thing
that got me most excited was what I…

00:01:07.747 --> 00:01:09.082
didn’t know.

00:01:09.082 --> 00:01:14.210
I feel like most people have some vague sense
that tardigrades are cool, and that single

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.991
celled organisms, exist…

00:01:16.991 --> 00:01:22.044
but when I was watching James’ videos, I
never knew what I was looking at.

00:01:22.044 --> 00:01:25.159
I didn’t have any categories to place these
things into.

00:01:25.159 --> 00:01:29.920
I felt like I was watching unnarrated footage
from a nature documentary…

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:35.673
of another planet, and that is a feeling
that I liked quite a lot.

00:01:35.673 --> 00:01:42.340
Luckily, James decided to let us join in on
his journey, sharing his videos and knowledge

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.873
so that we could pull more people into the
microcosmos.

00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:51.269
But James’ journey was already well 
underway before we found his content,

00:01:51.269 --> 00:01:54.587
and today we want to learn 
more about that path.

00:01:54.587 --> 00:01:59.460
Because here on Journey to the Microcosmos,
we’ve had the chance to explore the history

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:04.358
of microscopy through the lives of former
masters.

00:02:04.358 --> 00:02:08.850
But microscopy isn’t some ancient field
contained only in dusty books.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:14.819
It is still evolving through the work of
students and scientists and hobbyists who

00:02:14.819 --> 00:02:19.570
are finding new paths through microscopy today.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:24.580
For James, love began maybe where 
you’d least expect it.

00:02:28.967 --> 00:02:32.555
James: I was taking a lab course on wastewater
treatment.

00:02:32.555 --> 00:02:36.137
Yes, I know it sounds disgusting, and it was.

00:02:36.137 --> 00:02:40.800
We had jars full of human waste, and then
we had to look at those samples under the

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.430
microscope to see what creatures were growing
in there.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:47.080
And I thought it was going to be boring.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:52.692
But then I put a drop of that slimy stuff
onto a slide, placed the coverslip on top,

00:02:52.692 --> 00:02:55.181
and looked at it under the microscope.

00:02:55.181 --> 00:02:58.518
And it was like love at first sight.

00:02:58.518 --> 00:03:00.530
I couldn’t believe what I was seeing.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:05.459
I remember seeing something that looked like
a worm with a chainsaw mouth, which took me

00:03:05.459 --> 00:03:07.420
completely by surprise.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:14.030
I had no idea what I was looking at, but later
I learned it was a rotifer.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:19.120
I went into the course knowing absolutely
nothing about microbes, but I came out of

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:24.190
it knowing that a few hours of microscopy
in a class weren’t enough for me.

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:30.409
I had to get my own microscope, even though
it was a big expense for a poor student.

00:03:30.409 --> 00:03:35.739
And while I waited for it to arrive, I spent
hours and hours studying everything I could

00:03:35.739 --> 00:03:37.382
find about microbes.

00:03:40.966 --> 00:03:45.770
Hank: Many of the organisms that we get to
watch with James come from the Polish ponds

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:47.670
close to where he lives.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:52.200
But the first sample he ever studied under
his own microscope came from a puddle close

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.538
to his home.

00:03:53.538 --> 00:04:00.120
And the video of peritrich ciliates that we
opened the video with, that is the first thing

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:01.632
he recorded on it.

00:04:03.637 --> 00:04:07.988
James: I have so much from that first week
of microscopy to look back on.

00:04:07.988 --> 00:04:14.532
I had learned enough about peritrich ciliates
in my course work to identify them in my first slide.

00:04:14.532 --> 00:04:18.961
But other creatures were harder for me, like
this little green guy.

00:04:18.961 --> 00:04:25.831
I spent all night looking up pictures of algae
only to accidentally stumble on an organism

00:04:25.831 --> 00:04:32.500
I’d never heard of before called a desmid,
and after hours of Googling, I realized that

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:34.649
this was a desmid.

00:04:34.649 --> 00:04:39.800
It’s funny remembering how much time it
took me to realize I was looking at a desmid

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.254
because they are super common.

00:04:42.254 --> 00:04:49.210
Finding one and identifying it is like identifying
a cow in a field full of cows.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:54.620
But I found really unusual things in that
first week of microscopy, like these giant

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:59.080
purple amoeba that I haven’t been able to
find again since.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:04.139
And then there were odd things like these
Thecamoeba that I didn’t even think were

00:05:04.139 --> 00:05:05.880
microbes at first.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:11.583
It was all so confusing to understand, but
it became easier and easier the more I learned.

00:05:13.087 --> 00:05:16.676
Hank: These early videos, you know, they 
might not be James’ finest work.

00:05:16.676 --> 00:05:21.370
He’s holding a camera close to the eyepiece
with his hand, so sometimes things are a little

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:22.990
shaky or blurry.

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:26.798
So for those of us who have gotten used to
the clarity of his footage,

00:05:26.798 --> 00:05:29.166
this is a different experience.

00:05:29.166 --> 00:05:32.840
But that’s what makes these older videos
for me so fascinating.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:38.370
The work James produces now is so incredible
to look at that it’s easy to take the work

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:43.324
going into them for granted, to assume that
the microcosmos simply looks like that all

00:05:43.324 --> 00:05:44.599
of the time.

00:05:44.599 --> 00:05:50.450
The fascinating organisms he finds, the vivid
tableaus they paint across our screen—

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:52.919
those belong to the microcosmos.

00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:58.810
But they’re revealed to us through skills
that James has been honing for hours and hours

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:03.104
every day since he studied 
these first early slides.

00:06:03.104 --> 00:06:09.710
Those are hours that build up into years of
finding the right places to sample, of devising

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:14.509
new strategies to house those samples and
keep the organisms inside them alive.

00:06:14.509 --> 00:06:20.419
And then hours spent fine-tuning his skills
on the microscope, understanding what techniques

00:06:20.419 --> 00:06:25.479
to use to bring different layers of the microcosmos
to life.

00:06:30.354 --> 00:06:33.913
James: I think microscopy provides me with
creative ideas.

00:06:33.913 --> 00:06:35.440
Everything becomes a question of

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:37.580
“Can I use this in microscopy?”

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:43.200
For example, I heard once that hydras are
often found in aquariums.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:48.996
So I went to some pet stores, bought some
aquatic plants, and set up my first microbe tank.

00:06:48.996 --> 00:06:51.550
The aquatic plants came with some snails.

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:57.630
One day when I was looking at a snail under
the microscope, I found this green hydra stretching-off

00:06:57.630 --> 00:06:59.443
of the snail’s shell!

00:06:59.443 --> 00:07:03.900
And that’s the beauty of the microcosmos,
you can find the things you’re looking for

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:07.040
in places you’d never expect to find them.

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:12.139
And later, I created a YouTube channel to
share some of my videos with my professor,

00:07:12.139 --> 00:07:16.136
and I realized that I love recording videos
of microbes.

00:07:16.136 --> 00:07:19.740
I’m kind of like a hoarder of microscopy
footage.

00:07:21.934 --> 00:07:23.539
Hank: James isn’t kidding about that…

00:07:23.539 --> 00:07:28.199
he estimates that he has more than 5000 hours
of footage now.

00:07:28.199 --> 00:07:36.710
You could watch all ten Fast and Furious movies
269 times in 5,000 hours, and despite Vin

00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:42.669
Diesel’s best intentions, it would probably
not produce nearly as much drama and heartbreak

00:07:42.669 --> 00:07:44.880
as what James has recorded.

00:07:47.086 --> 00:07:50.608
James: The more I learned, the more excited
I got about microbes.

00:07:50.608 --> 00:07:54.000
I contacted scientists to start volunteering
in labs.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.819
I shared more videos on YouTube.

00:07:55.819 --> 00:07:58.099
I posted what I found on reddit.

00:07:58.099 --> 00:08:02.857
And then one day, one of my videos went viral
and that’s how Hank found me.

00:08:03.684 --> 00:08:08.590
Hank: And sharing his work helped James find
one of his most important collaborators and mentors:

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:14.319
Dr. Genoveva Esteban, a professor in microbiology
at Bournemouth University who has been studying

00:08:14.319 --> 00:08:16.343
ciliates for decades.

00:08:16.343 --> 00:08:21.949
Ciliates are single-celled eukaryotes known
for the hair cilia that cover their bodies,

00:08:21.949 --> 00:08:25.102
and James had become particularly interested
in them.

00:08:25.102 --> 00:08:30.610
Ciliates are a very, very diverse group of
organisms, including everything from this

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:34.919
Dactylochlamys to our favorite trumpet-like
Stentors.

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:39.959
And it turned out that thanks to his persistent
sampling, James had a real knack for finding

00:08:39.959 --> 00:08:41.804
very rare ciliates.

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:50.123
James: In one of my favorite places, I found
this weird ciliate.

00:08:50.123 --> 00:08:54.630
I didn’t know that Professor Esteban was
following my work, but she messaged me to

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:58.336
ask me about the super rare microbe I was
finding.

00:08:58.336 --> 00:09:02.680
And even though she has been in this field
for so long, she had never seen a species

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:03.956
like this.

00:09:03.956 --> 00:09:08.909
So we started texting about microbes, and
over time, we talked more and more about rare

00:09:08.909 --> 00:09:12.040
microbes and the interesting ciliates we were
finding.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:15.000
And now we even publish papers together.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:20.079
When I found the extremely rare Legendrea
loyezae, she was the first person I called.

00:09:20.079 --> 00:09:23.707
I remember pacing around 
in circles in my living room,

00:09:23.707 --> 00:09:26.641
telling her all about the tentacles extending.

00:09:28.471 --> 00:09:33.880
Hank: There are always all sorts of lessons
you can take from a story like James’.

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:38.434
His persistence, his love for his craft, the
joy of microscopy.

00:09:38.434 --> 00:09:41.480
But there’s something else too: community.

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:47.440
So much of James’ story is about being alone,
hours spent hunched over a microscope.

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:51.980
But so much of it is also about not being
alone, about finding people who share his

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:58.399
love for the microcosmos, and even people
who don’t know yet that they share that love.

00:09:58.399 --> 00:10:02.970
There are people he talks to every day about
microbes like Professor Esteban.

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:07.649
There are people he regales in meetings with
funny stories about the absurd lengths he’s

00:10:07.649 --> 00:10:12.850
gone to for his microbes, like those of us
on the Journey to the Microcosmos team.

00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:16.990
And there are even people he doesn’t know,
like many of you.

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:22.500
With every video, we get to see through his
eyes a little bit and journey to another world,

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:28.446
individuals brought together by a beam of
light wielded by a master’s hands.

00:10:36.519 --> 00:10:42.410
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:45.606
If you are interested in beginning your own
microscopy journey,

00:10:45.606 --> 00:10:48.000
you can head over to microcosmos.store

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:53.329
where we’ve got all of the stuff you need
to get started including microscopes, slides,

00:10:53.329 --> 00:10:57.459
and even a micro bumper sticker that says
“Tardigrade Enthusiast” so you can let

00:10:57.459 --> 00:11:01.480
everyone around you know that if they’re
out there searching for a person who shares

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:05.642
their love of the Microcosmos, you are that
person.

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:14.950
And speaking of people who share that love,
 these on the screen right

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:18.330
now are the names of our Patreon patrons.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:23.550
These are the folks who have made it possible
for us to go on this wonderful journey that,

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:26.930
I have been so happy to have been included
on.

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:31.689
So thank you to all of them for being such
a huge part of what we do.

00:11:31.689 --> 00:11:35.800
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes, James Weiss, you can check out

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:38.180
Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:42.791
And if you want to see more from us, there's
always a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

